from lib import *
C=10;S='maths'
# ---- Relations and functions ----
lesson(C,S,'relations-and-functions','cartesian-product-and-relations',"Ordered pairs, Cartesian product and relations","The seating chart","A class has 3 boys and 2 girls. The teacher wants to list every possible boy-girl pair for a dance. How many pairs are there, and how do we write them neatly? Mathematics has a tool for exactly this.",
["An ordered pair (a, b) is a pair where the order matters, so (2, 5) is not the same as (5, 2). @ ordered pair",
 "For two sets A and B, the Cartesian product A x B is the set of all ordered pairs (a, b) with a in A and b in B. @ A x B",
 "If A = {1, 2} and B = {x, y, z}, then A x B = {(1,x), (1,y), (1,z), (2,x), (2,y), (2,z)}. @ A x B",
 "If A has m elements and B has n elements, then A x B has m x n elements, so here 2 x 3 = 6. @ m x n",
 "In general A x B is not equal to B x A, unless A = B or one of them is empty. @ A x B not B x A",
 "A relation R from A to B is any subset of A x B. We pick the pairs that obey some rule. @ relation",
 "The set of first elements of the pairs is the domain, the whole set B is the codomain, and the set of second elements actually used is the range. @ domain and range",
 "A relation from A to B can be shown by an arrow diagram, a table, a graph or as a set of ordered pairs. @ arrow diagram"],
("If A = {1, 2, 3} and B = {2, 4}, write A x B and the relation R = {(a, b): b = 2a}.",[("List every pair: A x B = {(1,2),(1,4),(2,2),(2,4),(3,2),(3,4)}. That is 3 x 2 = 6 pairs.","Take each element of A and pair it with each element of B."),("Keep the pairs where b = 2a: (1,2) works because 2 = 2 x 1, and (2,4) works because 4 = 2 x 2.","Test each pair against the rule."),("So R = {(1,2),(2,4)}, domain = {1,2}, range = {2,4}.","Domain is the set of first elements, range the set of second elements used.")]),
("Explain in your own words what a Cartesian product is and how many pairs A x B has.",["All ordered pairs with first from A and second from B","Number of pairs is m x n","A relation is a subset of A x B"],["Thinking (a,b) and (b,a) are the same"]),
["An ordered pair cares about the order of its two entries.","A x B has n(A) x n(B) elements.","A relation is a subset of A x B; domain = first elements, range = second elements used.","A x B and B x A are usually different."],
["m|If n(A) = 4 and n(B) = 3, how many elements are in A x B?|12|7~You added instead of multiplying;9~Multiply 4 by 3, not 3 by 3;1|The number of pairs is 4 x 3 = 12.",
 "t|(2, 5) and (5, 2) are the same ordered pair.|False|Order matters, so they are different pairs unless the two entries are equal.",
 "m|A relation from A to B is|a subset of A x B|the whole of B~B is the codomain, not the relation;a number~A relation is a set of pairs|It is any subset of A x B.",
 "n|If A = {1,2,3} and B = {a,b}, how many elements has B x A?|6|B x A also has 2 x 3 = 6 pairs.",
 "t|A x B always equals B x A.|False|For example A = {1}, B = {2} gives (1,2) and (2,1), which differ.",
 "m|For R = {(1,2),(2,4),(3,6)} the range is|{2,4,6}|{1,2,3}~That is the domain;{1,2,3,4,6}~Only second elements form the range|Range is the set of second elements.",
 "n|If A = {1,2,3,4}, B = {5,6}, then n(A x B) =|8|4 x 2 = 8.",
 "s|The set of all first elements of a relation is called its (one word)|domain|The first elements form the domain."],
svg=(svg(460,230,'<ellipse cx="110" cy="115" rx="60" ry="85" fill="#aed6f1" stroke="#333"/><ellipse cx="350" cy="115" rx="60" ry="85" fill="#f9e79f" stroke="#333"/><text x="95" y="30" font-weight="bold">A</text><text x="340" y="30" font-weight="bold">B</text><text x="100" y="80">1</text><text x="100" y="125">2</text><text x="100" y="170">3</text><text x="340" y="95">2</text><text x="340" y="145">4</text><line x1="115" y1="76" x2="335" y2="91" stroke="#c0392b" stroke-width="2"/><line x1="115" y1="121" x2="335" y2="141" stroke="#c0392b" stroke-width="2"/><text x="150" y="215" font-size="14">Arrow diagram of R: b = 2a</text>'),"Arrow diagram from set A to set B showing 1 to 2 and 2 to 4","An arrow diagram of a relation"))

lesson(C,S,'relations-and-functions','functions-and-their-types',"What is a function? One-one, onto and constant functions","The vending machine","You press button B3 on a vending machine and always get the same biscuit. Press it again - same biscuit. A machine that gives exactly one output for each input is behaving like a function.",
["A function f from A to B is a relation in which every element of A is paired with exactly one element of B. @ function",
 "So no element of A may be left out and no element of A may have two arrows leaving it. @ one arrow each",
 "We write y = f(x); x is the independent variable (input) and y the dependent variable (output). @ y = f(x)",
 "A vertical-line test on a graph checks this: a vertical line must cut the graph at most once. @ vertical line test",
 "A function is one-one if different inputs always give different outputs, that is f(a) = f(b) implies a = b. @ one-one",
 "A function is onto if every element of the codomain is an output of some input, so range = codomain. @ onto",
 "A constant function gives the same output for every input, for example f(x) = 5. @ constant function",
 "An identity function maps every x to itself, f(x) = x, and a function can also be shown by a table, a set of pairs, an arrow diagram or a graph. @ identity function"],
("Is f = {(1,4),(2,5),(3,4)} a function from {1,2,3} to {4,5}? Is it one-one? Is it onto?",[("Each of 1, 2, 3 appears once as a first element, so f is a function.","Every input has exactly one output."),("But 1 and 3 both go to 4, so different inputs share an output. f is not one-one.","One-one needs different outputs for different inputs."),("The outputs used are 4 and 5, which is the whole codomain, so f is onto.","Range equals codomain.")]),
("How do you decide whether a relation is a function? Then explain one-one and onto.",["Each input has exactly one output","One-one means different inputs give different outputs","Onto means range equals codomain"],["Thinking two inputs sharing an output breaks the function rule"]),
["A function gives exactly one output for each input.","One-one: no two inputs share an output. Onto: range = codomain.","Constant function f(x) = c; identity function f(x) = x.","Use the vertical line test on graphs."],
["m|Which relation is NOT a function?|{(1,2),(1,3),(2,4)}|{(1,2),(2,3),(3,4)}~Each input appears once;{(1,5),(2,5),(3,5)}~A constant function is still a function|Input 1 has two outputs, 2 and 3.",
 "t|In a function, two different inputs can have the same output.|True|That is allowed; it only means the function is not one-one.",
 "m|f(x) = 7 for every x is called a|constant function|identity function~That would be f(x) = x;one-one function~All inputs give the same output|The output never changes.",
 "t|A one-one function must be onto.|False|One-one only needs different outputs; the range may still be smaller than the codomain.",
 "m|If f(x) = 2x + 1, then f(3) =|7|6~You forgot the + 1;5~Compute 2 x 3 + 1|2 x 3 + 1 = 7.",
 "n|If f(x) = x squared minus 1 (x^2 - 1), find f(4).|15|16 - 1 = 15.",
 "t|A vertical line cutting a graph twice means it is not a function graph.|True|One input would then have two outputs.",
 "s|f(x) = x is called the ____ function.|identity|It maps every number to itself."],
svg=(svg(460,230,'<ellipse cx="110" cy="115" rx="60" ry="85" fill="#aed6f1" stroke="#333"/><ellipse cx="350" cy="115" rx="60" ry="85" fill="#f9e79f" stroke="#333"/><text x="100" y="80">1</text><text x="100" y="125">2</text><text x="100" y="170">3</text><text x="340" y="95">4</text><text x="340" y="145">5</text><line x1="115" y1="76" x2="335" y2="91" stroke="#c0392b" stroke-width="2"/><line x1="115" y1="121" x2="335" y2="141" stroke="#c0392b" stroke-width="2"/><line x1="115" y1="166" x2="335" y2="95" stroke="#c0392b" stroke-width="2"/><text x="60" y="215" font-size="14">Function: every input has one arrow (not one-one)</text>'),"Arrow diagram of a function where 1 and 3 both map to 4","A function that is onto but not one-one"))

lesson(C,S,'relations-and-functions','composition-of-functions',"Composition of functions","The two-step machine","First machine doubles a number. Second machine adds 3. Put 5 in: 10 comes out of the first, then 13 from the second. Joining machines this way is called composition.",
["If f: A to B and g: B to C, the composition g o f maps x to g(f(x)). We do f first, then g. @ g o f",
 "Read g o f as g of f. The rightmost function acts first. @ right to left",
 "Example: f(x) = 2x and g(x) = x + 3. Then (g o f)(x) = g(2x) = 2x + 3. @ g(f(x))",
 "But (f o g)(x) = f(x + 3) = 2(x + 3) = 2x + 6, which is different. So composition is not commutative. @ not commutative",
 "Composition is associative: (h o g) o f = h o (g o f), so brackets do not change the result. @ associative",
 "The identity function I(x) = x leaves any function unchanged: f o I = I o f = f. @ identity",
 "If f o g = g o f = I, then g is the inverse of f, written f^-1. @ inverse",
 "Check the domains: the output of the first function must lie in the domain of the second. @ domain check"],
("If f(x) = 3x - 1 and g(x) = x squared, find (g o f)(2) and (f o g)(2).",[("f(2) = 3 x 2 - 1 = 5, so (g o f)(2) = g(5) = 25.","First f, then g."),("g(2) = 4, so (f o g)(2) = f(4) = 3 x 4 - 1 = 11.","First g, then f."),("25 and 11 differ, so g o f is not the same as f o g.","Order of composition matters.")]),
("Explain what g o f means and why the order matters.",["First apply f then g","g(f(x))","g o f is usually different from f o g"],["Applying g first because it is written first"]),
["(g o f)(x) = g(f(x)): f acts first.","Composition is associative but not commutative.","Identity function leaves a function unchanged.","If f o g = g o f = identity, they are inverses."],
["m|If f(x) = x + 2 and g(x) = 3x, then (g o f)(1) =|9|5~That is f(g(1)) = 5;3~Do f first: f(1) = 3, then g(3) = 9;6~Check each step|f(1) = 3 and g(3) = 9.",
 "t|In g o f, the function f is applied first.|True|g o f means g(f(x)).",
 "m|With f(x) = 2x and g(x) = x + 1, (f o g)(x) =|2x + 2|2x + 1~That is g o f;x + 2~Multiply the whole bracket by 2|f(x + 1) = 2(x + 1).",
 "n|f(x) = x + 4 and g(x) = 2x. Find (g o f)(3).|14|f(3) = 7 and g(7) = 14.",
 "t|Composition of functions is commutative.|False|f o g and g o f are usually different.",
 "t|(h o g) o f = h o (g o f).|True|Composition is associative.",
 "n|If f(x) = x squared and g(x) = x + 1, find (f o g)(2).|9|g(2) = 3, f(3) = 9.",
 "s|f o I = f. What is the function I called?|identity|I(x) = x."])
